WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:12.640
No one has ever painted snow the way Peder&nbsp;
Mønsted did more than a hundred years ago.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.760
In his paintings, you can almost feel&nbsp;
the cold. The low northern sun slips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.000
through the bare trees and sets the&nbsp;
snow alight, making it shine as if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.360
it had a light of its own. The shadows&nbsp;
turn blue and violet. And on the roads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.760
you can still see the tracks of a sleigh&nbsp;
that passed by just a few minutes ago.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.520
If you get close to any of his canvases, the&nbsp;
detail leaves you speechless. The frost on every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:44.080
branch, the reflection of the sky on the ice, the&nbsp;
snow piled up on the rooftops. It is all there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:49.520
with such sharpness that you wonder whether&nbsp;
you are looking at a painting or a photograph.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:55.920
During his lifetime, Mønsted achieved enormous&nbsp;
fame in Denmark. Kings and royal families across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.360
half of Europe hung his landscapes in their&nbsp;
palaces. And yet, if you visit the museums&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.400
of his country today, you will struggle&nbsp;
to find more than a handful of his works.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.840
But the question is, how could&nbsp;
a painter like this disappear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.120
from museums? And to answer it, we&nbsp;
have to go back to the beginning.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.000
Peder Mørk Mønsted was born in&nbsp;
1859 in Balle Mølle, a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.360
place near the coastal town of Grenaa, in Denmark.

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.080
He grew up in a well-off family. His&nbsp;
father built boats and worked as a miller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.640
until one day he took an unexpected turn&nbsp;
in his life and decided to earn his living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:38.800
as a photographer. With that change, the family&nbsp;
moved to Aarhus, the largest city in the region.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:45.520
In Aarhus, young Peder received his first painting&nbsp;
lessons from the landscape painter Andreas Fritz.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:49.120
And at only fifteen years old, in 1875,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.720
he walked through the doors of the Royal&nbsp;
Danish Academy of Fine Arts in Copenhagen.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:00.320
Those were turbulent years at the Academy. An&nbsp;
entire generation of young Danes was tired of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.400
traditional teaching, and several of&nbsp;
Mønsted’s classmates threw themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:10.320
into painting a realism charged with social&nbsp;
criticism... But he took a different path.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:16.480
In 1879 he left the Academy without sitting&nbsp;
for the final exam, and sought refuge in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.600
school of Peder Severin Krøyer, who at that&nbsp;
time was the most admired painter in Denmark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.000
and who had become famous for&nbsp;
capturing light in a masterful way.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:33.760
However, the experience that would define&nbsp;
his style came in the winter of 1882 to 1883.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:39.280
Mønsted went to Paris and worked for four months&nbsp;
in the studio of William-Adolphe Bouguereau,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.600
the French academic master we have talked&nbsp;
about several times on this channel.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.640
While the Impressionists were revolutionizing&nbsp;
the city with loose brushstrokes, Mønsted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:54.480
held on to the opposite. From Bouguereau he&nbsp;
learned the technique of academic naturalism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:00.000
an exact and meticulous way of painting&nbsp;
in which every detail is under control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.600
And that lesson would stay with&nbsp;
him for the rest of his career.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.040
Mønsted traveled across Europe and North&nbsp;
Africa with his brushes. He worked in Capri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:16.240
in Switzerland, and in Algeria. In 1892 he&nbsp;
traveled to Greece as the guest of King George I,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:21.760
a monarch who had been born a Danish prince, and&nbsp;
he ended up painting for the Greek royal family.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.600
His paintings also made their way onto the walls&nbsp;
of the family of King Christian IX of Denmark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:32.720
whose children and grandchildren sat on thrones&nbsp;
across the continent. The King of Greece, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:38.640
example, lived surrounded by Mønsted’s landscapes,&nbsp;
which reminded him of the land where he was born.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.280
Meanwhile, his works traveled to the great&nbsp;
exhibitions of Europe: the Exposition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:50.640
Universelle in Paris in 1889, the Glaspalast&nbsp;
in Munich in 1892, and the Charlottenborg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:57.280
exhibition in Copenhagen, where he took part&nbsp;
almost every year from 1879 until his death.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.840
By the turn of the century, his name was&nbsp;
already counted among the most celebrated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:07.200
artists in Denmark. He lived a life of&nbsp;
luxury, and in his final years he worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.880
in an enormous studio in Fredensborg, close&nbsp;
to the summer residence of the royal family.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:16.800
But now let us get to what&nbsp;
made him unique: his winters.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:22.720
Painting snow is a challenge that has defeated&nbsp;
many artists. Whoever paints it simply white ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.320
up with a flat, lifeless surface. Real snow&nbsp;
works almost like a mirror, and it takes on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:34.000
the color of everything around it, like the&nbsp;
sky, the sun, or the shadows of the trees.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.160
Mønsted knew this very well.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.640
You only need to look at one of his&nbsp;
winter landscapes. Where the sun falls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.600
the snow takes on warm, almost golden&nbsp;
tones. And in the shaded areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:51.200
it cools down until it turns blue and violet,&nbsp;
as if it were holding a piece of the sky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:56.000
Thanks to that contrast, his snow&nbsp;
has volume, temperature, and light.

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:01.360
The Dahesh Museum in New York, which holds one&nbsp;
of his snowy scenes, describes his style as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:07.840
“virtuoso naturalism.” According to the museum,&nbsp;
Mønsted combined the meticulous brushwork of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:13.040
the Danish tradition with a sensitivity to&nbsp;
light close to that of the Impressionists.

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:16.560
In that same work, the museum&nbsp;
highlights another detail:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.640
the small figures making their way&nbsp;
along the snowy road evoke one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.360
the first great winter landscapes in&nbsp;
European art, The Hunters in the Snow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:31.840
which Pieter Bruegel the Elder painted in 1565,&nbsp;
and which we have also analyzed on this channel.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:35.760
Mønsted was therefore part of a long&nbsp;
tradition of artists who tried to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:41.760
capture winter on canvas. And yet, very few&nbsp;
of them managed to do it with such precision.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:46.080
And it is at this point that his&nbsp;
story takes an unexpected turn.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:52.320
Mønsted passed away in 1941, at the age&nbsp;
of eighty-one, after a career full of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:58.240
success. But in the decades that followed,&nbsp;
his presence in museums slowly faded away.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:03.280
The same painter who sold his works to kings&nbsp;
has very few paintings in Denmark’s public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:08.560
collections today. Almost all of his work&nbsp;
ended up scattered among private owners.

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:14.720
Some find the explanation in his own success.&nbsp;
Mønsted developed a kind of landscape model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:19.520
each one capable of capturing the essence&nbsp;
of a place, such as the Danish winter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:25.360
the Norwegian fjord, or the Italian coast.&nbsp;
That formula made him a favorite among buyers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.360
but it may have cost him the&nbsp;
respect of museums and critics.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.400
Auction houses, on the other hand,&nbsp;
never forgot him. Starting in the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:37.440
the value of his works rose significantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.720
and his large winter landscapes remain&nbsp;
among the most sought after by collectors.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:48.080
Museums may have turned their backs on him, and&nbsp;
critics may have doubted him. But standing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:53.280
front of one of his winters, the effect is the&nbsp;
same as it was more than a hundred years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.800
For a moment, we feel the cold on our face&nbsp;
and watch the sun make the snow sparkle.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.160
And achieving that with a few&nbsp;
brushes and a handful of colors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.840
is something very few have ever been able to do.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.320
I would love to read in the comments whether&nbsp;
you had already seen any of his paintings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.200
and what his winter landscapes make you feel.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.600
As always, if you would like to have one&nbsp;
